.header

.h1 Status

.h2 Intro

The statusses display the result of various scripts. That may be a a listing,
a status (up/down) or a value or percentage. 
The default scanning for up/down is a ping. However, if a script
.fixed status_<servername>.sh
is present, that script will be used to detemine the status.

The dashboard displays 4 columns:

- The status of servers, which may be up, down or exluded. The list is sorted; all down
servers are at the top and all excluded are at the bottom
- The status of the network. At the top is the availability of the Internet and the route
from the scanning device towards the Internet, and below the status of all network devices
sorted in the same maner as the servers.
- Dashboard of specific items. These are generated by scripts under
.fixed scan_scripts
that start with
.fixed dashboard_
- A set of textual listings.  These are generated by scripts under
.fixed scan_scripts
that start with
.fixed listing_
when they are called with
.fixed -s
as argument.

Listings provide a tile per listing script. For the tiles, the listing script is called with
.fixed -htm
as argument. 

.h2 Status scripts

A status script detemines the status 
of a server. If the status is up, the script must end with an exit status 0. If the status is
down, an exit status of 1 is expected. The scripts should be kept simple and should match 
your environment. As an example, the following script tests is the Citadel server is running 
on a host:

.pre
#!/bin/bash

if ssh aesopos ps -ef | grep -q 'citserver' ; then
    exit 0
else
    exit 1
fi
.pre

.h2 Dashboard script

A dashboard script produces output on the STDOUT. 
The first line defines the facility.
It has the form of:

.pre
facility:
.pre

The facility line is used as a title. The subsequent line have the form of

.pre
format;name;value;color1;color2
.pre

|------------------------------------------|
|.b Field| .b Meaning                      |
|------------------------------------------|
|        |.b format |  .b content          |
|        |---------------------------------|
| format |value     |  any text            |
|        |---------------------------------|
|        | pct      |  0 - 100             |
|------------------------------------------|
| name   | The variable name               |
|------------------------------------------|
| value  | Value, text or numeric          |
|------------------------------------------|
| color1 | Color or the value or the left  |
|        | part of the bar                 |
|------------------------------------------|
| color2 | Color of the right              |
|        | part of the bar                 |
|------------------------------------------|

An example output of such a  script would be:

.pre
Epson:
pct;black;32;black;grey
pct;yellow;36;yellow;grey
pct;magenta;37;magenta;grey
pct;cyan;37;cyan;grey
.pre

This will result in the following image in the third column of the dashboard:

.img HALF epsondash.xcf

.h2 Listing scripts

Listing scripts are used to fill the fourth column of the dashboard and to fill a 
tile on the listings page.  The script names start with 
.fixed listing_
and are located in de scan_script directory. Scripts are called with two possible 
arguments:

- 
.fixed -s
(short or summary)
- 
.fixed -htm
(html-part)

When called with
.fixed -s
the script should give a short summary of the listingin plain text. This will
be used for the fourth column of the dashboard. When called with
.fixed -htm
the script should give a HTML version, without headers, which will be used
to fill a tile on the listings page. If there is no ouptut, no tile will be created
for this script.  A simple example of such a script could be:

.pre
#!/bin/bash

if [ "$1" = "-s" ] ; then
    date
elif [ "$1" = "-htm" ] ; then
    echo "<h1>The date is:</h1>"
    date
fi
.pre


